## Part 08 - Database & Repository Standard

Version: 1.0

Project: Pool OS

Date: 2026-07-03

---

# Purpose

This document defines the database and repository standard for Pool OS.

All persistent data must follow this standard.

Pool OS uses

Flutter

↓

Riverpod

↓

Repository

↓

Drift ORM

↓

SQLite

---

# Philosophy

Database stores data.

Repository owns data access.

Provider owns workflow.

Widget owns presentation.

These responsibilities shall never overlap.

---

# Database Responsibilities

Database SHALL

Store data

Retrieve data

Maintain indexes

Execute migrations

Enforce constraints

Database SHALL NEVER

Contain business logic

Generate Coach advice

Calculate statistics

Determine match winners

Perform navigation

Update UI

---

# Repository Responsibilities

Repository SHALL

Perform CRUD operations

Map database rows to models

Handle transactions

Handle database exceptions

Return domain models

Repository SHALL NEVER

Access BuildContext

Call Navigator

Update Provider state

Generate business decisions

---

# Repository Pattern

Every feature must have its own repository.

Example

PlayerRepository

SessionRepository

MatchRepository

RackRepository

ShotRepository

EquipmentRepository

CoachRepository

StatisticsRepository

PracticeRepository

Repositories must not access each other directly.

---

# CRUD Rules

Every repository must support

Create

Read

Update

Delete

Soft Delete (if applicable)

Operations must be atomic.

---

# Read Rules

Never assume a query returns exactly one row.

Forbidden

single()

firstWhere()

without validation

Always validate query results.

GOOD

if (rows.isEmpty)

return null

GOOD

if (rows.length > 1)

throw DuplicateDataException

BAD

rows.single()

---

# firstWhere Rule

Every firstWhere()

must contain

orElse

Example

GOOD

items.firstWhere(
    condition,
    orElse: () => defaultValue,
)

BAD

items.firstWhere(condition)

---

# Query Rules

Every query must define

Filter

Sort

Limit (if necessary)

No ambiguous query is allowed.

---

# Transactions

Use transactions whenever

Multiple inserts

Multiple updates

Multiple deletes

Related operations

Example

Create Match

↓

Create First Rack

↓

Update Session

↓

Commit

All or nothing.

---

# Foreign Keys

Foreign keys are mandatory.

Every relationship must be explicit.

Player

↓

Session

↓

Match

↓

Rack

↓

Shot

Deleting parent data must define

Cascade

Restrict

Set Null

Never rely on default behavior.

---

# Soft Delete

Historical data must use soft delete.

Never permanently remove

Sessions

Matches

Racks

Practice history

Statistics history

Permanent delete is allowed only for

Temporary cache

Logs

Draft data

---

# Migration Rules

Every migration must

Increase version number

Contain upgrade logic

Never remove previous migration

Migration order must remain sequential.

Example

v1

↓

v2

↓

v3

↓

...

↓

v10

Never skip versions.

